WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.500


2
00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:08.000
[MUSIC PLAYING]

3
00:00:08.000 --> 00:01:43.000


4
00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:43.500
All right.

5
00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:50.490
I hope you all
had a good break--

6
00:01:50.490 --> 00:01:55.860
spring break, as they
call it, uncreatively.

7
00:01:55.860 --> 00:01:57.318
I certainly had one.

8
00:01:57.318 --> 00:01:58.110
Didn't do any work.

9
00:01:58.110 --> 00:01:58.777
That's for sure.

10
00:01:58.777 --> 00:02:02.340


11
00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:06.000
Why would they put
this philosophy class

12
00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:08.639
in an engineering building?

13
00:02:08.639 --> 00:02:11.500
Hold on one moment, please.

14
00:02:11.500 --> 00:02:15.270
I'm speaking, and I will get
to you when it's your turn.

15
00:02:15.270 --> 00:02:23.070
I am Professor Breck Knowles.

16
00:02:23.070 --> 00:02:26.130
This is Advanced Ethics.

17
00:02:26.130 --> 00:02:29.505
You should be upper-class
philosophy majors.

18
00:02:29.505 --> 00:02:32.070


19
00:02:32.070 --> 00:02:35.130
That should be
all right in here.

20
00:02:35.130 --> 00:02:37.500
Can you please wait your turn?

21
00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:38.000
Please.

22
00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:40.710


23
00:02:40.710 --> 00:02:44.410
This is the textbook.

24
00:02:44.410 --> 00:02:48.630
Now, this textbook covers some
of the most important topics

25
00:02:48.630 --> 00:02:52.680
across ethics, edited, of
course, by yours truly.

26
00:02:52.680 --> 00:02:54.420
[CHUCKLES]

27
00:02:54.420 --> 00:03:00.720
I expect you all to have
it, and you can pull that--

28
00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:03.965
none of you have it.

29
00:03:03.965 --> 00:03:10.100
[SIGHS] The bookstore must have
really messed up this time.

30
00:03:10.100 --> 00:03:10.820
No.

31
00:03:10.820 --> 00:03:15.410
When you do get it, make sure
you get the new latest edition.

32
00:03:15.410 --> 00:03:20.150
I don't get my cut
for used books.

33
00:03:20.150 --> 00:03:27.350
Now, if you turn the page
432, that's my chapter.

34
00:03:27.350 --> 00:03:28.040
Could you read?

35
00:03:28.040 --> 00:03:31.246


36
00:03:31.246 --> 00:03:34.390
[CLEARS THROAT]
Chapter 18, "Bias

37
00:03:34.390 --> 00:03:36.460
is Bad," by Brack Know-less.

38
00:03:36.460 --> 00:03:37.755
Knowles!

39
00:03:37.755 --> 00:03:39.917
It's Knowles.

40
00:03:39.917 --> 00:03:44.500
[SIGHS] You surely don't know
how to impress your professors.

41
00:03:44.500 --> 00:03:47.470
You read.

42
00:03:47.470 --> 00:03:51.100
When investigating the
societal implications of bias

43
00:03:51.100 --> 00:03:54.460
within the framework of
non-hierarchical dependencies,

44
00:03:54.460 --> 00:03:57.910
we must integrate that the
taxonomy of bias and inequity

45
00:03:57.910 --> 00:04:00.250
are combinationally intertwined.

46
00:04:00.250 --> 00:04:05.350
First and foremost, bias affects
both the sub- super-conscious--

47
00:04:05.350 --> 00:04:07.060
Apologies for being late.

48
00:04:07.060 --> 00:04:08.692
Computer systems
ask me to help fix

49
00:04:08.692 --> 00:04:10.150
a glitch that
happened this morning

50
00:04:10.150 --> 00:04:13.480
and affected which classrooms
a few professors are sent to.

51
00:04:13.480 --> 00:04:14.260
Hi.

52
00:04:14.260 --> 00:04:15.330
I'm Sophie Muller.

53
00:04:15.330 --> 00:04:16.885
I don't think we've met.

54
00:04:16.885 --> 00:04:19.510
That's what I was trying to tell
Professor Know-less, that he's

55
00:04:19.510 --> 00:04:22.360
in the wrong classroom.

56
00:04:22.360 --> 00:04:24.010
My apologies.

57
00:04:24.010 --> 00:04:26.170
The problem with the software
upgrade this morning,

58
00:04:26.170 --> 00:04:27.790
but it's all fixed.

59
00:04:27.790 --> 00:04:29.650
This is not the advanced class?

60
00:04:29.650 --> 00:04:30.430
No.

61
00:04:30.430 --> 00:04:32.050
That's what I was
trying to tell you.

62
00:04:32.050 --> 00:04:34.360
We are in engineering ethics.

63
00:04:34.360 --> 00:04:40.160
Well, I always am supposed
to teach the advanced class.

64
00:04:40.160 --> 00:04:44.080
I don't want to be
stuck with beginners.

65
00:04:44.080 --> 00:04:46.540
But where am I supposed to go?

66
00:04:46.540 --> 00:04:47.260
Let's see.

67
00:04:47.260 --> 00:04:49.960
Knowles?

68
00:04:49.960 --> 00:04:52.840
Your class is all of the way
back in the philosophy floor

69
00:04:52.840 --> 00:04:56.680
in the subbasement of
building seven, room 001.

70
00:04:56.680 --> 00:04:57.910
A bit of a hike, I'm afraid.

71
00:04:57.910 --> 00:05:01.990
[CHUCKLES] Always get
stuck in the cave.

72
00:05:01.990 --> 00:05:04.910


73
00:05:04.910 --> 00:05:06.260
Excuse me.

74
00:05:06.260 --> 00:05:09.770
Do you want your book back?

75
00:05:09.770 --> 00:05:11.280
Thank you.

76
00:05:11.280 --> 00:05:15.140
You engineers can make something
that US philosophers deserve.

77
00:05:15.140 --> 00:05:16.047
Just--

78
00:05:16.047 --> 00:05:17.982
[LAUGHTER]

79
00:05:17.982 --> 00:05:18.482


80
00:05:18.482 --> 00:05:21.891
[MUSIC PLAYING]

81
00:05:21.891 --> 00:05:25.300


82
00:05:25.300 --> 00:05:27.530
How is everyone doing?

83
00:05:27.530 --> 00:05:28.030
Oh.

84
00:05:28.030 --> 00:05:30.380
I guess I didn't introduce
myself to all of you.

85
00:05:30.380 --> 00:05:31.600
I'm Sophie Muller.

86
00:05:31.600 --> 00:05:33.450
I'll be taking over
from Professor Baer, who

87
00:05:33.450 --> 00:05:35.200
has been asked to run
an important project

88
00:05:35.200 --> 00:05:37.270
on the ethics of AI.

89
00:05:37.270 --> 00:05:38.950
Did you have a
good spring break?

90
00:05:38.950 --> 00:05:41.110
Yeah.

91
00:05:41.110 --> 00:05:43.420
That's good, but the
registration list

92
00:05:43.420 --> 00:05:44.740
said 40 students.

93
00:05:44.740 --> 00:05:47.450
Where is everyone else?

94
00:05:47.450 --> 00:05:47.950
Yeah.

95
00:05:47.950 --> 00:05:50.590
Well, the others went to
go hear the president speak

96
00:05:50.590 --> 00:05:53.380
about the new administrative
organizational structure.

97
00:05:53.380 --> 00:05:55.270
He's changed it again.

98
00:05:55.270 --> 00:05:56.620
Third time this year.

99
00:05:56.620 --> 00:05:57.580
Mhm.

100
00:05:57.580 --> 00:05:59.560
The university president.

101
00:05:59.560 --> 00:06:00.670
Sounds interesting.

102
00:06:00.670 --> 00:06:01.930
[CHUCKLING]

103
00:06:01.930 --> 00:06:04.180
But I thought students almost
never go to such things.

104
00:06:04.180 --> 00:06:04.810
Yes.

105
00:06:04.810 --> 00:06:07.392
But since no one goes to
these and the president

106
00:06:07.392 --> 00:06:09.850
didn't want to speak in front
of an empty auditorium again,

107
00:06:09.850 --> 00:06:12.430
he offered everyone
free food and drink

108
00:06:12.430 --> 00:06:14.200
and allowed them
to break the rules

109
00:06:14.200 --> 00:06:15.910
and eat and drink
in the auditorium.

110
00:06:15.910 --> 00:06:18.460
He also hired a large
steam cleaning crew,

111
00:06:18.460 --> 00:06:20.380
complete with industrial
drying equipment

112
00:06:20.380 --> 00:06:22.090
to clean up right afterwards.

113
00:06:22.090 --> 00:06:23.390
I see.

114
00:06:23.390 --> 00:06:23.890
Oh.

115
00:06:23.890 --> 00:06:25.540
I realized that I
introduced myself

116
00:06:25.540 --> 00:06:27.940
but got distracted
by our conversation

117
00:06:27.940 --> 00:06:29.860
and didn't have you
introduce yourselves.

118
00:06:29.860 --> 00:06:31.510
What are your names?

119
00:06:31.510 --> 00:06:32.440
Alyssa.

120
00:06:32.440 --> 00:06:33.400
I'm Patch.

121
00:06:33.400 --> 00:06:34.420
My name is Sophie.

122
00:06:34.420 --> 00:06:35.000
Alvis.

123
00:06:35.000 --> 00:06:35.500
Glenda.

124
00:06:35.500 --> 00:06:36.370
Pleased to meet you.

125
00:06:36.370 --> 00:06:38.770
Well, nice to meet
all of you too.

126
00:06:38.770 --> 00:06:41.770
So, you were telling me about
the event going on right now.

127
00:06:41.770 --> 00:06:45.610
But why aren't you five there?

128
00:06:45.610 --> 00:06:46.630
Yes, Sophie.

129
00:06:46.630 --> 00:06:49.150
Well, professor, don't
tell anyone else,

130
00:06:49.150 --> 00:06:52.840
but we're really
dedicated to understanding

131
00:06:52.840 --> 00:06:55.850
the ethical issues
of bias in AI.

132
00:06:55.850 --> 00:06:58.030
In fact, instead of
traveling over spring break,

133
00:06:58.030 --> 00:07:00.760
we just stayed here to
discuss ethics altogether.

134
00:07:00.760 --> 00:07:06.520
And we looked at the resources
that we could find online.

135
00:07:06.520 --> 00:07:08.080
Your dedication is admirable.

136
00:07:08.080 --> 00:07:10.460
What did you find
in your research?

137
00:07:10.460 --> 00:07:13.090
Well, there are a bunch
of videos and articles

138
00:07:13.090 --> 00:07:15.400
online, but--

139
00:07:15.400 --> 00:07:16.150
But what?

140
00:07:16.150 --> 00:07:20.020
We found them unsatisfying.

141
00:07:20.020 --> 00:07:22.110
Why is that?

142
00:07:22.110 --> 00:07:22.610
Alyssa.

143
00:07:22.610 --> 00:07:25.510
Well, not everyone
found them unsatisfying.

144
00:07:25.510 --> 00:07:27.100
I thought they made
some good points.

145
00:07:27.100 --> 00:07:29.950
Well, you weren't able to
explain away their failings.

146
00:07:29.950 --> 00:07:30.550
Were you?

147
00:07:30.550 --> 00:07:32.710
No, but you weren't able
to give a better approach.

148
00:07:32.710 --> 00:07:33.490
Were you?

149
00:07:33.490 --> 00:07:34.510
Hold on, you two.

150
00:07:34.510 --> 00:07:35.440
Always bickering.

151
00:07:35.440 --> 00:07:37.780
We didn't answer
professor's question.

152
00:07:37.780 --> 00:07:41.260
Professor Muller, we found
them unsatisfying because they

153
00:07:41.260 --> 00:07:42.820
didn't give us an answer.

154
00:07:42.820 --> 00:07:43.480
That's right.

155
00:07:43.480 --> 00:07:45.110
They told us that
it's a problem,

156
00:07:45.110 --> 00:07:46.930
and they said that
it's a hard problem,

157
00:07:46.930 --> 00:07:50.030
but they didn't tell
us how to address it.

158
00:07:50.030 --> 00:07:51.340
It's more than that.

159
00:07:51.340 --> 00:07:54.620
The videos on bias in
AI are all the same.

160
00:07:54.620 --> 00:07:57.190
They all say it's in
the data set-- bias in,

161
00:07:57.190 --> 00:08:00.460
bias out-- and they keep
recycling the same examples.

162
00:08:00.460 --> 00:08:03.430
Some videos have better
graphics or better speakers,

163
00:08:03.430 --> 00:08:05.470
but other than that,
they're all the same.

164
00:08:05.470 --> 00:08:08.120
[SCOFFS] So conformist.

165
00:08:08.120 --> 00:08:08.620
Yeah.

166
00:08:08.620 --> 00:08:11.440
And while they say that
bias is in the data set,

167
00:08:11.440 --> 00:08:13.850
there seems to be
no general solution.

168
00:08:13.850 --> 00:08:16.930
They just indicate that for
each algorithm the issue of bias

169
00:08:16.930 --> 00:08:19.060
needs to be researched.

170
00:08:19.060 --> 00:08:22.510
Even for that, they don't
give a systematic approach.

171
00:08:22.510 --> 00:08:23.140
Yeah.

172
00:08:23.140 --> 00:08:25.240
Even if you could choose
the right data set

173
00:08:25.240 --> 00:08:28.030
and quantify it on
that data set to ensure

174
00:08:28.030 --> 00:08:30.790
no bias in your training
set for a given analysis,

175
00:08:30.790 --> 00:08:34.510
you have no way to know if
that applies to all situations.

176
00:08:34.510 --> 00:08:37.510
My point is that we all
agree that bias is bad.

177
00:08:37.510 --> 00:08:40.450
We simply need to address
it in every single algorithm

178
00:08:40.450 --> 00:08:41.470
that we make.

179
00:08:41.470 --> 00:08:42.980
Yes, I agree.

180
00:08:42.980 --> 00:08:44.800
We don't want any
bias in our AI.

181
00:08:44.800 --> 00:08:46.640
We want everyone to be happy.

182
00:08:46.640 --> 00:08:48.640
This is a good start,
but perhaps it's

183
00:08:48.640 --> 00:08:50.470
best if we get to specifics.

184
00:08:50.470 --> 00:08:53.290
Alyssa, what were some of
the good points regarding

185
00:08:53.290 --> 00:08:55.300
algorithmic bias
that you mentioned?

186
00:08:55.300 --> 00:08:57.400
For example,
algorithms are biased

187
00:08:57.400 --> 00:08:59.650
against underrepresented
minorities.

188
00:08:59.650 --> 00:09:01.840
Facial recognition doesn't
work as well for Blacks

189
00:09:01.840 --> 00:09:04.480
as it does for Whites,
especially Black women.

190
00:09:04.480 --> 00:09:06.760
One common example shows
that while it identifies

191
00:09:06.760 --> 00:09:09.910
White males nearly perfectly,
it misidentified Black woman

192
00:09:09.910 --> 00:09:11.980
over 20% of the time.

193
00:09:11.980 --> 00:09:16.480
Well, one algorithm gives
20% error and the other 30%.

194
00:09:16.480 --> 00:09:19.270
They can't even be
consistent in their bias.

195
00:09:19.270 --> 00:09:22.000
If we can't nail down the
extent of the problem,

196
00:09:22.000 --> 00:09:23.215
how do we fix it?

197
00:09:23.215 --> 00:09:25.590
You could at least work to
try to make sure that the data

198
00:09:25.590 --> 00:09:27.160
set is more representative.

199
00:09:27.160 --> 00:09:30.910
Oh, and what about the algorithm
that identifies Indian brides

200
00:09:30.910 --> 00:09:31.960
as actors?

201
00:09:31.960 --> 00:09:34.030
Well, why should they complain?

202
00:09:34.030 --> 00:09:37.630
Being identified as an actor
is the greatest honor anyone

203
00:09:37.630 --> 00:09:38.290
can have.

204
00:09:38.290 --> 00:09:39.710
Oh, very funny.

205
00:09:39.710 --> 00:09:41.230
I like chicken sandwiches.

206
00:09:41.230 --> 00:09:43.450
How would you like it if
an algorithm identified you

207
00:09:43.450 --> 00:09:44.570
as a chicken sandwich?

208
00:09:44.570 --> 00:09:45.070
OK.

209
00:09:45.070 --> 00:09:48.190
I see why they call it
an adversarial example.

210
00:09:48.190 --> 00:09:50.800
In any case, it's not
easy to address bias

211
00:09:50.800 --> 00:09:52.480
across the board
because of skews

212
00:09:52.480 --> 00:09:54.880
in data sets due to
historical inequities.

213
00:09:54.880 --> 00:09:56.260
And those skews differ.

214
00:09:56.260 --> 00:09:58.990
At least we know that there
have been historical inequities

215
00:09:58.990 --> 00:10:00.500
that we need to address.

216
00:10:00.500 --> 00:10:03.310
And while it may
not be easy to do,

217
00:10:03.310 --> 00:10:07.360
we have to at least try, even
if it's on a case-by-case basis.

218
00:10:07.360 --> 00:10:09.520
Even if it is on a
case-by-case basis,

219
00:10:09.520 --> 00:10:11.200
we're doomed to
miss a lot of bias

220
00:10:11.200 --> 00:10:13.180
until long after
everyone is equal.

221
00:10:13.180 --> 00:10:15.910
And I for one am
not that patient.

222
00:10:15.910 --> 00:10:18.370
Well, this problem
is so important

223
00:10:18.370 --> 00:10:21.370
that even if it takes a very
long time and even if we

224
00:10:21.370 --> 00:10:23.170
have to do it
algorithm by algorithm,

225
00:10:23.170 --> 00:10:24.700
that is what we should do.

226
00:10:24.700 --> 00:10:27.410
If we all do this,
everyone will be happy.

227
00:10:27.410 --> 00:10:29.230
But no, many people
won't be happy

228
00:10:29.230 --> 00:10:31.460
waiting for the bias to go away.

229
00:10:31.460 --> 00:10:35.050
So, professor, this is where
we got stuck over spring break.

230
00:10:35.050 --> 00:10:37.060
It's just not
practical to engineer

231
00:10:37.060 --> 00:10:39.220
every algorithm against bias.

232
00:10:39.220 --> 00:10:41.710
Even if we could, we would
end up eliminating it

233
00:10:41.710 --> 00:10:43.000
in a few dimensions.

234
00:10:43.000 --> 00:10:46.390
But then we're bound to miss it
in other important dimensions.

235
00:10:46.390 --> 00:10:48.490
Above and beyond
that, we can't even

236
00:10:48.490 --> 00:10:52.000
quantify the bias consistently
since different studies

237
00:10:52.000 --> 00:10:55.660
on the same data set give
different amounts of bias.

238
00:10:55.660 --> 00:10:58.210
We know that we need to do
something to address bias,

239
00:10:58.210 --> 00:11:02.425
but what the videos say will at
best take a small step forward.

240
00:11:02.425 --> 00:11:04.300
We know that others may
be content with that,

241
00:11:04.300 --> 00:11:07.900
but it's not sufficient for us
to feel good about ourselves.

242
00:11:07.900 --> 00:11:09.080
No, it's not.

243
00:11:09.080 --> 00:11:09.580
Ah.

244
00:11:09.580 --> 00:11:12.710
You have reached an impasse,
which, believe it or not,

245
00:11:12.710 --> 00:11:16.660
is often a necessary place to
be before you can make progress.

246
00:11:16.660 --> 00:11:19.930
I think, however, it's best if
we take a step back and explore

247
00:11:19.930 --> 00:11:22.690
how these algorithms work
and the roots of wanting

248
00:11:22.690 --> 00:11:23.980
to eliminate bias.

249
00:11:23.980 --> 00:11:27.382
[MUSIC PLAYING]

250
00:11:27.382 --> 00:11:35.660


251
00:11:35.660 --> 00:11:39.080
So you probably all know
this, but just to remind you,

252
00:11:39.080 --> 00:11:41.272
the overall approach is simple.

253
00:11:41.272 --> 00:11:42.980
You start out with a
training set of data

254
00:11:42.980 --> 00:11:46.700
here together with descriptors,
which we use to derive a model.

255
00:11:46.700 --> 00:11:49.490
Then we input the data,
and the model classifies it

256
00:11:49.490 --> 00:11:51.200
based on some criterion.

257
00:11:51.200 --> 00:11:53.750
Obviously, the devil
is in the details.

258
00:11:53.750 --> 00:11:55.520
Based on this
high-level approach,

259
00:11:55.520 --> 00:11:58.420
where do you see bias?

260
00:11:58.420 --> 00:12:00.250
There's no bias here.

261
00:12:00.250 --> 00:12:03.520
The algorithm-- say, a
convolutional neural network--

262
00:12:03.520 --> 00:12:06.820
objectively analyzes
the data provided to it.

263
00:12:06.820 --> 00:12:07.720
It's just math.

264
00:12:07.720 --> 00:12:09.280
It is not just math.

265
00:12:09.280 --> 00:12:10.930
We choose the training set.

266
00:12:10.930 --> 00:12:13.060
We choose the algorithm.

267
00:12:13.060 --> 00:12:14.920
Even if we think that
we are not biased,

268
00:12:14.920 --> 00:12:17.080
we are subconsciously biased.

269
00:12:17.080 --> 00:12:19.150
How do we actually
choose them if everything

270
00:12:19.150 --> 00:12:21.100
is described by mathematics?

271
00:12:21.100 --> 00:12:23.350
We don't really have a choice.

272
00:12:23.350 --> 00:12:24.040
Duh.

273
00:12:24.040 --> 00:12:27.670
The bias is already in the data
due to historical inequity.

274
00:12:27.670 --> 00:12:28.990
You said it yourself.

275
00:12:28.990 --> 00:12:31.420
So then our choice is
to note that and add

276
00:12:31.420 --> 00:12:33.220
an appropriate loss function.

277
00:12:33.220 --> 00:12:35.360
Does everybody know
what a loss function is?

278
00:12:35.360 --> 00:12:36.780
Of course.

279
00:12:36.780 --> 00:12:37.320
Yes.

280
00:12:37.320 --> 00:12:37.950
Yeah.

281
00:12:37.950 --> 00:12:40.110
A loss function is
just the error function

282
00:12:40.110 --> 00:12:42.990
associated with an event,
such as facial recognition.

283
00:12:42.990 --> 00:12:45.060
It is what should be
mathematically minimized.

284
00:12:45.060 --> 00:12:46.200
Good.

285
00:12:46.200 --> 00:12:49.350
Just to clarify, it
shouldn't be simply minimized

286
00:12:49.350 --> 00:12:52.510
across the board but
targeted, for example,

287
00:12:52.510 --> 00:12:54.900
so that the overall error
in facial recognition

288
00:12:54.900 --> 00:12:56.700
is minimized with the
constraint that it is

289
00:12:56.700 --> 00:12:58.600
equal among different groups.

290
00:12:58.600 --> 00:13:00.720
But unless you have
equivalent training sets

291
00:13:00.720 --> 00:13:03.090
for the different groups,
the statistical errors

292
00:13:03.090 --> 00:13:04.230
won't be equivalent.

293
00:13:04.230 --> 00:13:06.570
And you can't quantify
the errors in advance

294
00:13:06.570 --> 00:13:09.220
to determine whether or not
they will be equivalent.

295
00:13:09.220 --> 00:13:12.300
In other words, you can't choose
the loss function approach

296
00:13:12.300 --> 00:13:16.020
to solve the problem because
the fluctuations or randomness.

297
00:13:16.020 --> 00:13:18.270
The problem is statistics.

298
00:13:18.270 --> 00:13:20.490
We have to have
a very large data

299
00:13:20.490 --> 00:13:23.820
set to average out randomness
to acceptably small percentages

300
00:13:23.820 --> 00:13:27.220
and make sure that our
data is representative.

301
00:13:27.220 --> 00:13:30.390
Yes, but my point is that
it's just not possible.

302
00:13:30.390 --> 00:13:32.010
I agree that we
could make the error

303
00:13:32.010 --> 00:13:33.990
rates in facial
recognition algorithms

304
00:13:33.990 --> 00:13:37.540
equal for Whites and Blacks
and for men and women.

305
00:13:37.540 --> 00:13:40.470
But what about all the other
racial and ethnic minorities?

306
00:13:40.470 --> 00:13:43.350
And for that matter, what about
all the different genders?

307
00:13:43.350 --> 00:13:45.600
Even if we could
agree on what groups

308
00:13:45.600 --> 00:13:48.510
need to have bias eliminated,
we can't practically

309
00:13:48.510 --> 00:13:49.680
address them all.

310
00:13:49.680 --> 00:13:52.290
Yeah, and that's not
even how the field works.

311
00:13:52.290 --> 00:13:55.620
No one's going to spend five
or 10 years for each algorithm

312
00:13:55.620 --> 00:13:57.180
to eliminate bias.

313
00:13:57.180 --> 00:13:59.010
For cutting-edge
research, people just

314
00:13:59.010 --> 00:14:03.300
try to hit 80%, sometimes 90%
accuracy for a given algorithm

315
00:14:03.300 --> 00:14:05.100
and then publish a paper on it.

316
00:14:05.100 --> 00:14:07.590
The incentives are such
that they can't spend years

317
00:14:07.590 --> 00:14:11.520
getting 99% or so accuracy
across the board, at least

318
00:14:11.520 --> 00:14:13.020
for new algorithms.

319
00:14:13.020 --> 00:14:15.060
There just isn't enough data.

320
00:14:15.060 --> 00:14:16.840
Better to just get
something out there,

321
00:14:16.840 --> 00:14:20.460
even if it's not so good, so
you at least get credit for it.

322
00:14:20.460 --> 00:14:22.500
If we could only
eliminate randomness,

323
00:14:22.500 --> 00:14:24.390
our problems would be solved.

324
00:14:24.390 --> 00:14:28.890
But randomness is good because
it's the basis of choice,

325
00:14:28.890 --> 00:14:32.250
otherwise our minds would just
follow a deterministic path

326
00:14:32.250 --> 00:14:35.190
based on our neural pathways
and electrical signals.

327
00:14:35.190 --> 00:14:37.120
The heart of it is
quantum mechanics,

328
00:14:37.120 --> 00:14:39.450
which introduces
intrinsic randomness.

329
00:14:39.450 --> 00:14:43.230
Well, randomness doesn't
give you choice, just chaos.

330
00:14:43.230 --> 00:14:44.910
That's also the
reason we have bias

331
00:14:44.910 --> 00:14:48.660
to begin with, because of
random historical circumstances.

332
00:14:48.660 --> 00:14:52.110
We want to eliminate bias,
not just make it random.

333
00:14:52.110 --> 00:14:54.600
If it is random, then
maybe you will mitigate it

334
00:14:54.600 --> 00:14:57.450
for minorities, or
maybe, at least for some,

335
00:14:57.450 --> 00:14:59.100
you'll find a way to augment it.

336
00:14:59.100 --> 00:15:02.130
[SCOFFS] That is what
I was saying, too.

337
00:15:02.130 --> 00:15:04.530
So we seem to be stuck again.

338
00:15:04.530 --> 00:15:07.440
Mathematics is the rigorous
way to understand the world,

339
00:15:07.440 --> 00:15:09.390
but it doesn't give us choice.

340
00:15:09.390 --> 00:15:11.250
In the world described
by mathematics,

341
00:15:11.250 --> 00:15:13.710
choice is an illusion.

342
00:15:13.710 --> 00:15:15.750
Professor, we ran
into the same impasse

343
00:15:15.750 --> 00:15:19.020
when we had this discussion
last week during spring break.

344
00:15:19.020 --> 00:15:21.460
But how do we get out of it?

345
00:15:21.460 --> 00:15:23.770
I think I can be
of some help here.

346
00:15:23.770 --> 00:15:28.200
So you all agree that it is
desirable to eliminate bias,

347
00:15:28.200 --> 00:15:29.110
right?

348
00:15:29.110 --> 00:15:29.610
Yeah.

349
00:15:29.610 --> 00:15:30.110
Yes.

350
00:15:30.110 --> 00:15:33.120
So you all think it is possible
to mitigate bias depending

351
00:15:33.120 --> 00:15:34.600
on what actions you take?

352
00:15:34.600 --> 00:15:35.100
Yeah.

353
00:15:35.100 --> 00:15:36.060
Yeah.

354
00:15:36.060 --> 00:15:40.110
So you do think that can make
choices and not just randomly.

355
00:15:40.110 --> 00:15:42.060
More well-grounded
choices are better than

356
00:15:42.060 --> 00:15:45.840
less well-grounded ones,
say, for addressing bias.

357
00:15:45.840 --> 00:15:48.990
I don't think we really
have a choice about things.

358
00:15:48.990 --> 00:15:52.050
People might think that they
do, but they don't really.

359
00:15:52.050 --> 00:15:55.830
We can't choose where we are
born or how smart we are,

360
00:15:55.830 --> 00:15:58.980
and we can't choose if
we get hit by a car.

361
00:15:58.980 --> 00:16:03.520
We think we are making a
choice, but we really aren't.

362
00:16:03.520 --> 00:16:04.780
It's like Sophie said.

363
00:16:04.780 --> 00:16:06.430
It's all just an illusion.

364
00:16:06.430 --> 00:16:07.180
Yeah.

365
00:16:07.180 --> 00:16:08.260
Yeah, you're right.

366
00:16:08.260 --> 00:16:10.060
Randomness doesn't help.

367
00:16:10.060 --> 00:16:11.630
You've convinced me.

368
00:16:11.630 --> 00:16:12.130
Wait.

369
00:16:12.130 --> 00:16:15.080
Did I have a choice, or
was I compelled to agree?

370
00:16:15.080 --> 00:16:15.580
No.

371
00:16:15.580 --> 00:16:18.130
Your neurons told you to agree.

372
00:16:18.130 --> 00:16:19.570
Yes, exactly.

373
00:16:19.570 --> 00:16:24.490
And randomness, a consequence
of a chaotic quantum process,

374
00:16:24.490 --> 00:16:26.800
just makes what we
do not predictable

375
00:16:26.800 --> 00:16:29.620
but doesn't mean that
we choose what we do.

376
00:16:29.620 --> 00:16:30.670
Right.

377
00:16:30.670 --> 00:16:34.300
Well, you have a choice whether
you're going to be biased

378
00:16:34.300 --> 00:16:35.230
or not.

379
00:16:35.230 --> 00:16:35.830
Mhm.

380
00:16:35.830 --> 00:16:37.040
Indeed.

381
00:16:37.040 --> 00:16:39.580
It does seem that
judgments involve choice.

382
00:16:39.580 --> 00:16:42.280
But Alyssa, if you were
given the full understanding

383
00:16:42.280 --> 00:16:45.070
of a situation, wouldn't you
always take the best action?

384
00:16:45.070 --> 00:16:46.510
Yeah, of course.

385
00:16:46.510 --> 00:16:47.260
No.

386
00:16:47.260 --> 00:16:50.530
You can't really choose whether
anything is better or worse.

387
00:16:50.530 --> 00:16:52.540
It's all just a value judgment.

388
00:16:52.540 --> 00:16:54.940
You may be compelled to
do something by others,

389
00:16:54.940 --> 00:16:58.067
but you never really
freely choose.

390
00:16:58.067 --> 00:16:59.650
Do you really believe
that, or are you

391
00:16:59.650 --> 00:17:02.000
just playing devil's advocate?

392
00:17:02.000 --> 00:17:02.500
OK.

393
00:17:02.500 --> 00:17:03.710
A little of both.

394
00:17:03.710 --> 00:17:06.400
But even if I am just
playing devil's advocate,

395
00:17:06.400 --> 00:17:07.960
what's wrong with
what I'm saying?

396
00:17:07.960 --> 00:17:12.280
What's wrong is that your model
of human life is inadequate.

397
00:17:12.280 --> 00:17:14.619
It doesn't describe
what it really is.

398
00:17:14.619 --> 00:17:17.260
We're not just some balls
moving around deterministically

399
00:17:17.260 --> 00:17:20.740
with random noise interspersed
like a term in a Langevin

400
00:17:20.740 --> 00:17:22.329
equation.

401
00:17:22.329 --> 00:17:24.579
Isn't that true,
Professor Muller?

402
00:17:24.579 --> 00:17:26.550
What a great discussion this is.

403
00:17:26.550 --> 00:17:28.300
Sounds like you had
a much better spring

404
00:17:28.300 --> 00:17:31.180
break discussing these questions
than going to the beach.

405
00:17:31.180 --> 00:17:32.410
[CHUCKLES]

406
00:17:32.410 --> 00:17:34.370
I think you're on the
right track, Sophie.

407
00:17:34.370 --> 00:17:34.870
Funny.

408
00:17:34.870 --> 00:17:38.050
Coincidentally, that's
my first name too.

409
00:17:38.050 --> 00:17:40.750
At any rate, you have
unveiled a great tension

410
00:17:40.750 --> 00:17:42.580
in modern science.

411
00:17:42.580 --> 00:17:45.520
We are brought up to think
that modern science is the way

412
00:17:45.520 --> 00:17:47.890
to describe the
world, but it seems

413
00:17:47.890 --> 00:17:50.230
inadequate to
describe human things.

414
00:17:50.230 --> 00:17:53.290
Ethical questions cannot be
reducible to mathematics--

415
00:17:53.290 --> 00:17:57.680
similar to justice and
beauty, hope and suffering,

416
00:17:57.680 --> 00:18:00.970
and, for that
matter, even truth.

417
00:18:00.970 --> 00:18:02.290
Yes, Glenda.

418
00:18:02.290 --> 00:18:05.860
Aren't those things even more
important than modern science?

419
00:18:05.860 --> 00:18:07.300
I would say so.

420
00:18:07.300 --> 00:18:08.020
No.

421
00:18:08.020 --> 00:18:11.080
Those things are
arbitrary or a matter

422
00:18:11.080 --> 00:18:14.480
of individual's interests--
what's in it for them.

423
00:18:14.480 --> 00:18:16.900
That's the way to look
at them scientifically.

424
00:18:16.900 --> 00:18:20.490
Bias exists because people
have conflicting interests.

425
00:18:20.490 --> 00:18:22.780
Sure, if you look at it
purely scientifically,

426
00:18:22.780 --> 00:18:25.600
but interest alone cannot
be adequate since people are

427
00:18:25.600 --> 00:18:28.720
willing to sacrifice interests
for passions and, more

428
00:18:28.720 --> 00:18:31.690
importantly, for things
beyond themselves.

429
00:18:31.690 --> 00:18:34.240
At any rate, do most
of us really know

430
00:18:34.240 --> 00:18:35.800
all of our interests?

431
00:18:35.800 --> 00:18:39.380
We all have preferences that
go far beyond interests.

432
00:18:39.380 --> 00:18:43.390
Perhaps we can say at best that
those interests are intertwined

433
00:18:43.390 --> 00:18:45.700
with different
approaches to the world.

434
00:18:45.700 --> 00:18:49.090
Besides, aren't the most
boring people the ones who say,

435
00:18:49.090 --> 00:18:52.660
I'm right, and, even if one
can never make value judgments,

436
00:18:52.660 --> 00:18:54.910
I have my own values
and I stick with them?

437
00:18:54.910 --> 00:18:58.630
[CHUCKLES] Yeah, these are
the most irritating and boring

438
00:18:58.630 --> 00:18:59.920
people.

439
00:18:59.920 --> 00:19:04.030
So, now let's dig down a bit
more into the mathematics

440
00:19:04.030 --> 00:19:06.400
of AI and the sorting problem.

441
00:19:06.400 --> 00:19:09.910
Perhaps it's best to start
with a specific example.

442
00:19:09.910 --> 00:19:13.270
What's the reason for the
bias in facial recognition

443
00:19:13.270 --> 00:19:15.040
that we discussed
a little while ago?

444
00:19:15.040 --> 00:19:18.210
A biased training set due
to historical inequity.

445
00:19:18.210 --> 00:19:18.970
OK.

446
00:19:18.970 --> 00:19:21.280
So one of the issues
is the training set.

447
00:19:21.280 --> 00:19:23.200
The other issue
is the algorithm,

448
00:19:23.200 --> 00:19:25.540
including its hyperparameters,
all leading to how

449
00:19:25.540 --> 00:19:27.400
the classification is done.

450
00:19:27.400 --> 00:19:30.070
This is why different algorithms
give different error rates

451
00:19:30.070 --> 00:19:32.050
using the same training data.

452
00:19:32.050 --> 00:19:33.010
Anything else?

453
00:19:33.010 --> 00:19:36.270


454
00:19:36.270 --> 00:19:36.960
Right.

455
00:19:36.960 --> 00:19:39.670
So those are the two key issues.

456
00:19:39.670 --> 00:19:42.000
However, within the
choice of algorithm,

457
00:19:42.000 --> 00:19:44.530
there is the problem
with classification.

458
00:19:44.530 --> 00:19:46.680
Let me project
this data set, here

459
00:19:46.680 --> 00:19:48.450
with points in red and blue.

460
00:19:48.450 --> 00:19:52.030
You have many choices for
putting the dividing surface.

461
00:19:52.030 --> 00:19:54.510
But what if we
choose the wrong one?

462
00:19:54.510 --> 00:19:57.510
Some of the data may be
misclassified, won't it?

463
00:19:57.510 --> 00:19:58.500
You mean like this?

464
00:19:58.500 --> 00:20:00.180
Yes, exactly.

465
00:20:00.180 --> 00:20:04.390
So how do you choose the
right line ahead of time?

466
00:20:04.390 --> 00:20:06.420
That is a problem.

467
00:20:06.420 --> 00:20:10.310
It could be even worse if
your data set looks like this.

468
00:20:10.310 --> 00:20:12.970
But then we don't have
to stick with lines.

469
00:20:12.970 --> 00:20:14.620
Why don't you draw
a curve around them?

470
00:20:14.620 --> 00:20:15.370
Like this?

471
00:20:15.370 --> 00:20:17.200
Yes, exactly.

472
00:20:17.200 --> 00:20:19.210
Now you've classified
them all perfectly.

473
00:20:19.210 --> 00:20:20.173
Well, wait a second.

474
00:20:20.173 --> 00:20:22.090
If you do that, it works
for the training set,

475
00:20:22.090 --> 00:20:24.580
but it may not work for
any additional data sets.

476
00:20:24.580 --> 00:20:27.010
In fact, it might be
even worse than the lines

477
00:20:27.010 --> 00:20:29.350
because you're overfitting
to the training data.

478
00:20:29.350 --> 00:20:31.090
Do you mean like this?

479
00:20:31.090 --> 00:20:32.050
Yeah.

480
00:20:32.050 --> 00:20:33.280
That doesn't work.

481
00:20:33.280 --> 00:20:34.840
Oh, yes.

482
00:20:34.840 --> 00:20:35.740
I see it.

483
00:20:35.740 --> 00:20:36.790
Exactly.

484
00:20:36.790 --> 00:20:39.250
Remember this is a sorting
algorithm, here in two

485
00:20:39.250 --> 00:20:42.925
dimensions with two descriptors,
thus it is quite limited.

486
00:20:42.925 --> 00:20:45.550
But even if you do it in a very
large number of dimensions with

487
00:20:45.550 --> 00:20:47.470
a very large number
of descriptors,

488
00:20:47.470 --> 00:20:49.810
you are still projecting
a complicated system--

489
00:20:49.810 --> 00:20:51.910
shall we say a natural system--

490
00:20:51.910 --> 00:20:54.610
onto an artificial
mathematical construct

491
00:20:54.610 --> 00:20:58.030
that necessarily leaves
much out of the description.

492
00:20:58.030 --> 00:21:01.390
Or if you want to stay in
the realm of mathematics,

493
00:21:01.390 --> 00:21:04.150
you could say that due to
a necessary incomplete set

494
00:21:04.150 --> 00:21:07.990
of descriptors you
were fitting noise.

495
00:21:07.990 --> 00:21:11.230
This allows you to get a perfect
fit for your training set,

496
00:21:11.230 --> 00:21:13.570
but then you have an even
worse fit for your data set

497
00:21:13.570 --> 00:21:15.780
moving forward.

498
00:21:15.780 --> 00:21:17.400
Patch.

499
00:21:17.400 --> 00:21:19.890
In other words, we
have to accept the fact

500
00:21:19.890 --> 00:21:21.790
that there will be errors.

501
00:21:21.790 --> 00:21:24.960
But at least we could distribute
the errors evenly among groups.

502
00:21:24.960 --> 00:21:27.340
That is what Alyssa said before.

503
00:21:27.340 --> 00:21:29.940
We can try, but
then I'm worried we

504
00:21:29.940 --> 00:21:32.110
won't include all the groups.

505
00:21:32.110 --> 00:21:35.460
Even if we could, there'll
be errors in the errors.

506
00:21:35.460 --> 00:21:37.590
Unless we have a very,
very large training

507
00:21:37.590 --> 00:21:40.500
set, which practically
we never have,

508
00:21:40.500 --> 00:21:42.120
we won't be able to do that.

509
00:21:42.120 --> 00:21:42.960
Yes.

510
00:21:42.960 --> 00:21:44.790
The training set would
have to incorporate

511
00:21:44.790 --> 00:21:46.350
all the data in the world.

512
00:21:46.350 --> 00:21:48.390
You know, that
reminds me of a story

513
00:21:48.390 --> 00:21:51.030
I once heard, I think from
a South American writer,

514
00:21:51.030 --> 00:21:53.820
where someone makes a one-to-one
scale map of their country

515
00:21:53.820 --> 00:21:55.740
with all the details included.

516
00:21:55.740 --> 00:21:56.550
[CHUCKLES]

517
00:21:56.550 --> 00:21:58.980
Ha, ha.

518
00:21:58.980 --> 00:22:00.570
Well, I grant the problem.

519
00:22:00.570 --> 00:22:03.550
But then what do we do?

520
00:22:03.550 --> 00:22:06.570
We all agree that we
need to eliminate bias,

521
00:22:06.570 --> 00:22:10.800
but it seems so hard.

522
00:22:10.800 --> 00:22:15.060
What if we could at least
have a very extensive model

523
00:22:15.060 --> 00:22:20.260
that for all practical purposes
captures a given situation?

524
00:22:20.260 --> 00:22:23.700
Which I guess is
actually impossible

525
00:22:23.700 --> 00:22:26.700
because it would require a
complete and accurate model

526
00:22:26.700 --> 00:22:29.100
of all the objects
in the whole world.

527
00:22:29.100 --> 00:22:29.800
Yes.

528
00:22:29.800 --> 00:22:32.550
As engineers, we can at
least work to do that.

529
00:22:32.550 --> 00:22:36.750
If the model is extensive
enough, we can eliminate bias.

530
00:22:36.750 --> 00:22:40.470
But then we'd be eliminating any
choice that we might have had.

531
00:22:40.470 --> 00:22:43.650
We would be controlled
by an algorithm.

532
00:22:43.650 --> 00:22:49.200
That sounds-- that
sounds tyrannical.

533
00:22:49.200 --> 00:22:49.980
Indeed.

534
00:22:49.980 --> 00:22:53.220
Mathematization of human
things distorts them so much

535
00:22:53.220 --> 00:22:55.680
that they become something
which they are not.

536
00:22:55.680 --> 00:22:58.500
This is actually the
utilitarian approach.

537
00:22:58.500 --> 00:23:03.690
Human things like joy, anger,
justice, honor, beauty,

538
00:23:03.690 --> 00:23:07.170
and happiness are really
not mathematizable.

539
00:23:07.170 --> 00:23:09.150
We lose all of what
is important in them

540
00:23:09.150 --> 00:23:11.160
when we mathematize them.

541
00:23:11.160 --> 00:23:15.390
We could in principle try to
do so and therein transform

542
00:23:15.390 --> 00:23:16.155
human reality.

543
00:23:16.155 --> 00:23:19.260


544
00:23:19.260 --> 00:23:20.370
Glenda.

545
00:23:20.370 --> 00:23:22.680
That doesn't sound appealing.

546
00:23:22.680 --> 00:23:24.990
It sounds dreadful.

547
00:23:24.990 --> 00:23:27.090
Yeah, that wouldn't be good.

548
00:23:27.090 --> 00:23:30.570
But we can at least do
it partially, can't we?

549
00:23:30.570 --> 00:23:31.260
Yes.

550
00:23:31.260 --> 00:23:33.060
Maybe that would
be a good solution.

551
00:23:33.060 --> 00:23:33.960
Yes.

552
00:23:33.960 --> 00:23:36.120
Even if those human
things cannot be described

553
00:23:36.120 --> 00:23:39.600
by mathematics, it is still the
only rigorous way to understand

554
00:23:39.600 --> 00:23:40.320
the world.

555
00:23:40.320 --> 00:23:45.300
I'm not so sure anymore, but
the bias is in the data set,

556
00:23:45.300 --> 00:23:47.310
and it is in the algorithms.

557
00:23:47.310 --> 00:23:52.050
So we must be able to adjust
our models to solve the problem.

558
00:23:52.050 --> 00:23:53.370
Mustn't we?

559
00:23:53.370 --> 00:23:56.700
Well, consider this-- our
recognition of the problem

560
00:23:56.700 --> 00:23:58.860
of bias is not mathematical.

561
00:23:58.860 --> 00:24:02.400
Mathematics comes only
after such recognition.

562
00:24:02.400 --> 00:24:04.800
Mathematics is something
we impose on our broader

563
00:24:04.800 --> 00:24:06.550
understanding of the world.

564
00:24:06.550 --> 00:24:09.300
We can use it to develop
models, but they will always

565
00:24:09.300 --> 00:24:12.120
be just that, models
which are necessarily

566
00:24:12.120 --> 00:24:14.280
incomplete descriptions.

567
00:24:14.280 --> 00:24:20.400
I see what you're
saying, I think.

568
00:24:20.400 --> 00:24:21.390
Sophie.

569
00:24:21.390 --> 00:24:24.310
But AI is based on mathematics.

570
00:24:24.310 --> 00:24:27.600
So we have to fix this
problem in the algorithms

571
00:24:27.600 --> 00:24:30.240
via fixing the algorithms, i.e.

572
00:24:30.240 --> 00:24:31.680
by using mathematics.

573
00:24:31.680 --> 00:24:32.880
Don't we?

574
00:24:32.880 --> 00:24:35.160
Well, we can think
of things this way,

575
00:24:35.160 --> 00:24:37.950
but that will lead us
to a double whammy.

576
00:24:37.950 --> 00:24:40.170
We cannot develop a
non-arbitrary definition

577
00:24:40.170 --> 00:24:43.800
of the criteria for the
training set, and, two,

578
00:24:43.800 --> 00:24:46.290
we cannot develop a
non-arbitrary definition

579
00:24:46.290 --> 00:24:50.850
of the error target for
the sorting algorithm.

580
00:24:50.850 --> 00:24:53.340
Well, I knew that we
were at an impasse,

581
00:24:53.340 --> 00:24:56.070
but I didn't think it
was this big of one.

582
00:24:56.070 --> 00:24:57.540
Professor, your
class has made us

583
00:24:57.540 --> 00:24:59.920
more stuck than we were before.

584
00:24:59.920 --> 00:25:02.640
So, what do we do?

585
00:25:02.640 --> 00:25:04.740
Is there another way?

586
00:25:04.740 --> 00:25:06.400
Indeed, Glenda, there is that.

587
00:25:06.400 --> 00:25:10.633
It requires a reconsideration of
our concerns and of the issue.

588
00:25:10.633 --> 00:25:14.084
[MUSIC PLAYING]

589
00:25:14.084 --> 00:25:25.916


590
00:25:25.916 --> 00:25:27.395
[DOOR CLOSES]

591
00:25:27.395 --> 00:25:40.230


592
00:25:40.230 --> 00:25:40.760
Thank you.

593
00:25:40.760 --> 00:25:44.550


594
00:25:44.550 --> 00:25:45.720
Sophie.

595
00:25:45.720 --> 00:25:48.280
So, what is the other way?

596
00:25:48.280 --> 00:25:49.710
Well, let's think about it.

597
00:25:49.710 --> 00:25:52.650
We just showed that because of
the intrinsic incompleteness

598
00:25:52.650 --> 00:25:55.170
of models eliminating
bias in all dimensions

599
00:25:55.170 --> 00:25:57.210
is mathematically impossible.

600
00:25:57.210 --> 00:26:00.120
We could no doubt decrease
bias among some groups

601
00:26:00.120 --> 00:26:03.540
to a certain degree, with an
acceptably small but still

602
00:26:03.540 --> 00:26:05.610
significant margin of error.

603
00:26:05.610 --> 00:26:08.520
Even if we got rid of bias in
some predefined dimensions,

604
00:26:08.520 --> 00:26:10.800
we would still have it
in other dimensions.

605
00:26:10.800 --> 00:26:12.900
But that's precisely it.

606
00:26:12.900 --> 00:26:15.840
We want equality in at
least some dimensions,

607
00:26:15.840 --> 00:26:17.040
the important ones.

608
00:26:17.040 --> 00:26:17.910
That is good.

609
00:26:17.910 --> 00:26:20.760
How do you choose which
dimensions are important?

610
00:26:20.760 --> 00:26:21.420
Alvis.

611
00:26:21.420 --> 00:26:23.730
One important
dimension is wealth.

612
00:26:23.730 --> 00:26:25.350
Good.

613
00:26:25.350 --> 00:26:28.500
So even you five, the
top engineering students

614
00:26:28.500 --> 00:26:31.320
in your class at the top
engineering school, should get

615
00:26:31.320 --> 00:26:35.130
paid just as much as someone
like Dean, the paper collector.

616
00:26:35.130 --> 00:26:40.350
Well, he is a very nice
fellow, if a bit strange.

617
00:26:40.350 --> 00:26:44.550
But no, we think we should be
rewarded for the contributions

618
00:26:44.550 --> 00:26:46.530
that we make to society.

619
00:26:46.530 --> 00:26:49.648
But wealth should be
distributed more equally.

620
00:26:49.648 --> 00:26:50.190
That's right.

621
00:26:50.190 --> 00:26:52.110
But how do you decide
how equal it should be?

622
00:26:52.110 --> 00:26:55.290


623
00:26:55.290 --> 00:26:57.240
Perhaps the problem
is once again

624
00:26:57.240 --> 00:27:00.360
that mathematics in its
homogenization of kinds

625
00:27:00.360 --> 00:27:03.090
is not the right way
to address the problem.

626
00:27:03.090 --> 00:27:04.650
Let me ask you this--

627
00:27:04.650 --> 00:27:07.920
is it really equality that
you want, or is it justice?

628
00:27:07.920 --> 00:27:08.760
Justice.

629
00:27:08.760 --> 00:27:09.450
Yeah.

630
00:27:09.450 --> 00:27:11.250
I'll go with justice.

631
00:27:11.250 --> 00:27:14.580
Great that you two
agree on something.

632
00:27:14.580 --> 00:27:15.720
Glenda.

633
00:27:15.720 --> 00:27:18.600
They should go hand in
hand, justice and equality.

634
00:27:18.600 --> 00:27:19.140
Yeah.

635
00:27:19.140 --> 00:27:20.970
Isn't equality justice?

636
00:27:20.970 --> 00:27:23.280
I think we need to add
another consideration,

637
00:27:23.280 --> 00:27:25.560
since we are beginning to
delve into the complex human

638
00:27:25.560 --> 00:27:27.600
questions underlying
our definition

639
00:27:27.600 --> 00:27:29.680
and perception of bias.

640
00:27:29.680 --> 00:27:30.850
So let me ask you this.

641
00:27:30.850 --> 00:27:33.780
Which is most important--
equality, justice,

642
00:27:33.780 --> 00:27:36.370
or happiness?

643
00:27:36.370 --> 00:27:37.540
Sophie.

644
00:27:37.540 --> 00:27:39.700
I would say happiness.

645
00:27:39.700 --> 00:27:41.450
Even if someone has
more than you do,

646
00:27:41.450 --> 00:27:43.360
and even if they
don't deserve it,

647
00:27:43.360 --> 00:27:46.540
it doesn't matter to you
as long as you are happy.

648
00:27:46.540 --> 00:27:49.270
Think of all the people
born into rich families.

649
00:27:49.270 --> 00:27:51.100
They're typically
not happy since they

650
00:27:51.100 --> 00:27:54.310
don't need to have a goal, and
therefore they don't have one.

651
00:27:54.310 --> 00:27:57.213
I would still have a goal if I
were born into a rich family.

652
00:27:57.213 --> 00:27:58.630
Why do they need
a goal if they're

653
00:27:58.630 --> 00:28:00.250
having such a good time?

654
00:28:00.250 --> 00:28:03.400
Because people need to
have a purpose to be happy.

655
00:28:03.400 --> 00:28:07.270
And how can people be happy
if they're not treated fairly?

656
00:28:07.270 --> 00:28:10.550
So we have equality,
justice, and happiness,

657
00:28:10.550 --> 00:28:13.674
but it is unclear which
is most important.

658
00:28:13.674 --> 00:28:14.620
Glenda.

659
00:28:14.620 --> 00:28:16.600
Can't they be of
equal importance?

660
00:28:16.600 --> 00:28:17.830
Actually, no.

661
00:28:17.830 --> 00:28:19.510
They are certainly interrelated.

662
00:28:19.510 --> 00:28:22.580
But since they are at
least somewhat different

663
00:28:22.580 --> 00:28:25.030
and since, as we saw, they
can conflict, one of them

664
00:28:25.030 --> 00:28:28.670
has to be most important, has to
take precedence over the other.

665
00:28:28.670 --> 00:28:31.600
I think that justice is more
important than equality.

666
00:28:31.600 --> 00:28:34.870
After all, we don't want
everyone to be the exact same,

667
00:28:34.870 --> 00:28:38.980
but still justice and
equality are related.

668
00:28:38.980 --> 00:28:42.220
People can be unequal because
they're treated unfairly.

669
00:28:42.220 --> 00:28:45.340
They can also be unequal
due to luck, good or bad.

670
00:28:45.340 --> 00:28:47.770
I don't think that happiness
can be the standard.

671
00:28:47.770 --> 00:28:50.860
We need equality, and that's
why mathematics is still

672
00:28:50.860 --> 00:28:53.800
so important, because we
can employ it rigorously

673
00:28:53.800 --> 00:28:55.510
to make everyone equal.

674
00:28:55.510 --> 00:28:59.350
But if everyone is the same,
wouldn't the world be drab?

675
00:28:59.350 --> 00:29:01.360
I mean, what if all
of us were the same?

676
00:29:01.360 --> 00:29:04.360
We wouldn't even be able to
have this dialogue, as we'd all

677
00:29:04.360 --> 00:29:05.992
be making the same point.

678
00:29:05.992 --> 00:29:07.450
There would be
nothing interesting,

679
00:29:07.450 --> 00:29:11.290
as everyone is reduced
to a forced homogeneity.

680
00:29:11.290 --> 00:29:13.120
Besides, how do
you make everyone

681
00:29:13.120 --> 00:29:15.340
equal without
violating their rights?

682
00:29:15.340 --> 00:29:17.710
We're going to have to
violate some rights to make

683
00:29:17.710 --> 00:29:19.460
people at least more equal.

684
00:29:19.460 --> 00:29:23.290
But then maybe I guess
that's not justice.

685
00:29:23.290 --> 00:29:23.920
Yeah.

686
00:29:23.920 --> 00:29:27.130
Isn't it the essence of tyranny
when rights are violated

687
00:29:27.130 --> 00:29:28.810
in the name of justice?

688
00:29:28.810 --> 00:29:29.680
Yes.

689
00:29:29.680 --> 00:29:32.050
We have strong reasons
not to want that.

690
00:29:32.050 --> 00:29:34.030
We want equality,
but we certainly

691
00:29:34.030 --> 00:29:37.290
don't want to violate
people's rights to get it.

692
00:29:37.290 --> 00:29:39.710
But what do we do?

693
00:29:39.710 --> 00:29:41.750
Here we are at an impasse again.

694
00:29:41.750 --> 00:29:43.400
How do we understand
this conflict

695
00:29:43.400 --> 00:29:46.430
between equality and rights?

696
00:29:46.430 --> 00:29:48.650
By the way, professor,
what about that other way

697
00:29:48.650 --> 00:29:49.670
that you promised?

698
00:29:49.670 --> 00:29:52.960
[MUSIC PLAYING]

699
00:29:52.960 --> 00:29:54.840


700
00:29:54.840 --> 00:29:57.455
I was wondering when you
would want to go to that.

701
00:29:57.455 --> 00:29:59.580
Do you remember learning
about modern natural right

702
00:29:59.580 --> 00:30:02.140
from Professor Baer
before spring break?

703
00:30:02.140 --> 00:30:03.430
Yes, a bit.

704
00:30:03.430 --> 00:30:05.970
We studied Locke's state of
nature and its connection

705
00:30:05.970 --> 00:30:09.120
with natural right, if that's
what you're talking about.

706
00:30:09.120 --> 00:30:10.740
Professor Baer
also made the case

707
00:30:10.740 --> 00:30:14.290
that modern natural right is the
basis for the American regime

708
00:30:14.290 --> 00:30:16.510
and the reason for
this country's success.

709
00:30:16.510 --> 00:30:17.010
Good.

710
00:30:17.010 --> 00:30:18.900
And yes, that is
exactly what I mean.

711
00:30:18.900 --> 00:30:20.400
It's great that you
remember reading

712
00:30:20.400 --> 00:30:22.020
Locke and about
modern natural right

713
00:30:22.020 --> 00:30:25.730
being the basis for
the American regime.

714
00:30:25.730 --> 00:30:26.230
Oh.

715
00:30:26.230 --> 00:30:27.630
Hello, Professor Knowles.

716
00:30:27.630 --> 00:30:28.890
What are you doing here?

717
00:30:28.890 --> 00:30:30.990
I hope I'm not bothering you.

718
00:30:30.990 --> 00:30:33.540
I heard that you were
discussing modern natural right,

719
00:30:33.540 --> 00:30:36.420
and I wanted to join.

720
00:30:36.420 --> 00:30:38.340
The stuff you heard
me spew earlier

721
00:30:38.340 --> 00:30:41.040
was a front that I
have to put forward

722
00:30:41.040 --> 00:30:43.470
to my colleagues in the
philosophy department,

723
00:30:43.470 --> 00:30:46.770
and some students who
appreciate such things.

724
00:30:46.770 --> 00:30:49.200
It's the only way I
could keep a job, which

725
00:30:49.200 --> 00:30:52.245
I need to be able to
pursue true philosophy.

726
00:30:52.245 --> 00:30:53.370
That is refreshing to hear.

727
00:30:53.370 --> 00:30:54.900
And yes, please do join us.

728
00:30:54.900 --> 00:30:58.110
We were just starting
on modern natural right.

729
00:30:58.110 --> 00:31:03.690
So how does Locke view the
problem of AI and bias?

730
00:31:03.690 --> 00:31:04.590
Patch.

731
00:31:04.590 --> 00:31:07.530
Locke would think it
wrong because bias

732
00:31:07.530 --> 00:31:09.600
violates your natural right.

733
00:31:09.600 --> 00:31:11.400
Yes, I agree.

734
00:31:11.400 --> 00:31:15.300
That sounds right,
but I'm not sure

735
00:31:15.300 --> 00:31:19.620
because we said that justice is
more important than equality.

736
00:31:19.620 --> 00:31:24.180
But it seems that equality
is fundamental in Locke.

737
00:31:24.180 --> 00:31:27.300
But then where is
justice in Locke?

738
00:31:27.300 --> 00:31:29.880
Let's take this step by step.

739
00:31:29.880 --> 00:31:31.500
Does anyone remember
the beginning?

740
00:31:31.500 --> 00:31:34.210


741
00:31:34.210 --> 00:31:36.210
But you said you were
working on these questions

742
00:31:36.210 --> 00:31:38.160
during spring break.

743
00:31:38.160 --> 00:31:38.760
Alvis?

744
00:31:38.760 --> 00:31:42.870
Well, we were discussing them,
but we never actually went back

745
00:31:42.870 --> 00:31:45.030
to the texts from class.

746
00:31:45.030 --> 00:31:45.600
Yeah.

747
00:31:45.600 --> 00:31:49.300
We just wanted to figure
them out for ourselves.

748
00:31:49.300 --> 00:31:52.560
I guess you disapprove,
Professor Muller?

749
00:31:52.560 --> 00:31:54.030
On the contrary.

750
00:31:54.030 --> 00:31:55.800
You are a very
impressive group to have

751
00:31:55.800 --> 00:31:57.870
stayed here over spring
break and investigated

752
00:31:57.870 --> 00:31:58.800
these questions.

753
00:31:58.800 --> 00:32:02.100
Any teacher would be thrilled to
have even one student like you,

754
00:32:02.100 --> 00:32:03.810
and I have five.

755
00:32:03.810 --> 00:32:06.000
But you said that you got stuck.

756
00:32:06.000 --> 00:32:08.010
What was your plan to
get out of your impasse?

757
00:32:08.010 --> 00:32:09.840
Well, your class, of course.

758
00:32:09.840 --> 00:32:11.070
[LAUGHS]

759
00:32:11.070 --> 00:32:12.150
Hush, Patch.

760
00:32:12.150 --> 00:32:15.000
You seem not to realize
that Professor Muller is

761
00:32:15.000 --> 00:32:17.520
in a most mild way
rebuking us for not

762
00:32:17.520 --> 00:32:19.680
using the resources at hand.

763
00:32:19.680 --> 00:32:21.900
Well, let's use them now.

764
00:32:21.900 --> 00:32:24.660
Professor, please remind us
of Locke's starting point.

765
00:32:24.660 --> 00:32:25.630
Of course.

766
00:32:25.630 --> 00:32:28.170
Let's turn to second
treatise, chapter 1.

767
00:32:28.170 --> 00:32:31.020


768
00:32:31.020 --> 00:32:33.850
Professor Knowles, where do
you think we should start?

769
00:32:33.850 --> 00:32:36.660
I'd say the last section
of paragraph one,

770
00:32:36.660 --> 00:32:38.820
starting with "he that."

771
00:32:38.820 --> 00:32:40.680
That is exactly
what I was thinking.

772
00:32:40.680 --> 00:32:42.480
Glenda, could you
read the statement

773
00:32:42.480 --> 00:32:44.550
starting with "he that?"

774
00:32:44.550 --> 00:32:45.790
Of course.

775
00:32:45.790 --> 00:32:47.820
He that will not
give just occasion

776
00:32:47.820 --> 00:32:49.560
to think that all
government in the world

777
00:32:49.560 --> 00:32:51.630
is the product of only
force and violence

778
00:32:51.630 --> 00:32:54.510
must of necessity find out
another rise of government.

779
00:32:54.510 --> 00:32:55.890
Good.

780
00:32:55.890 --> 00:32:58.320
So Locke is giving
us two options

781
00:32:58.320 --> 00:33:02.250
of the origin of government,
force or something else--

782
00:33:02.250 --> 00:33:04.290
maybe something
else based on reason

783
00:33:04.290 --> 00:33:07.110
but not narrow or
mathematical reason.

784
00:33:07.110 --> 00:33:08.310
Yes, of course.

785
00:33:08.310 --> 00:33:09.900
That is much better.

786
00:33:09.900 --> 00:33:12.360
It would be terrible to
think that politics is just

787
00:33:12.360 --> 00:33:13.320
about force.

788
00:33:13.320 --> 00:33:14.250
I agree.

789
00:33:14.250 --> 00:33:15.690
It should be about
allowing people

790
00:33:15.690 --> 00:33:18.390
to be happy, as we said before.

791
00:33:18.390 --> 00:33:22.400
But what does this
alternative basis?

792
00:33:22.400 --> 00:33:25.170
You can look at your
books, you know.

793
00:33:25.170 --> 00:33:25.670
Alvis.

794
00:33:25.670 --> 00:33:27.000
It is the state of nature.

795
00:33:27.000 --> 00:33:27.500
Good.

796
00:33:27.500 --> 00:33:29.360
What is the state of nature?

797
00:33:29.360 --> 00:33:30.260
Well, let's see.

798
00:33:30.260 --> 00:33:33.560
I actually underlined
this pretty extensively.

799
00:33:33.560 --> 00:33:37.910
It is a state of perfect freedom
and a state also of equality.

800
00:33:37.910 --> 00:33:38.660
Yes.

801
00:33:38.660 --> 00:33:40.130
I remember now.

802
00:33:40.130 --> 00:33:43.730
But how can it be a
state of perfect freedom

803
00:33:43.730 --> 00:33:47.540
if anyone can take your
stuff and it's only up to you

804
00:33:47.540 --> 00:33:49.430
to protect it?

805
00:33:49.430 --> 00:33:50.700
You don't remember?

806
00:33:50.700 --> 00:33:51.800
That's the point.

807
00:33:51.800 --> 00:33:53.570
There is no government,
so we're all

808
00:33:53.570 --> 00:33:55.430
perfectly free in that sense.

809
00:33:55.430 --> 00:33:57.980
But life is not
desirable since we're

810
00:33:57.980 --> 00:34:00.260
all individually vulnerable.

811
00:34:00.260 --> 00:34:03.020
That's why we form
governments, for security.

812
00:34:03.020 --> 00:34:05.700
Very good, Sophie, but
you're moving too fast.

813
00:34:05.700 --> 00:34:09.050
Remember Locke says but though
this be a state of liberty,

814
00:34:09.050 --> 00:34:11.420
yet it is not a
state of license.

815
00:34:11.420 --> 00:34:13.040
Do you recall why that is?

816
00:34:13.040 --> 00:34:14.090
Oh, hold on.

817
00:34:14.090 --> 00:34:15.230
I have it right here.

818
00:34:15.230 --> 00:34:18.590
Because of a law of nature.

819
00:34:18.590 --> 00:34:19.429
Yes, that's it.

820
00:34:19.429 --> 00:34:22.010
Laws of nature are
based on reason.

821
00:34:22.010 --> 00:34:22.639
Yeah.

822
00:34:22.639 --> 00:34:24.500
We talked about that
before spring break.

823
00:34:24.500 --> 00:34:25.699
Now I remember.

824
00:34:25.699 --> 00:34:29.333
It's dumb to waste stuff,
including your own life.

825
00:34:29.333 --> 00:34:31.250
What about these people
that waste their money

826
00:34:31.250 --> 00:34:33.800
on luxuries, expensive
cars and things?

827
00:34:33.800 --> 00:34:34.340
Yeah.

828
00:34:34.340 --> 00:34:36.620
I'd be content with
a cheap rundown car.

829
00:34:36.620 --> 00:34:38.870
Why should someone buy a
Ferrari when most of us

830
00:34:38.870 --> 00:34:40.340
can't afford an old Kia?

831
00:34:40.340 --> 00:34:44.530
Does anyone recall
Locke's answer to this?

832
00:34:44.530 --> 00:34:47.810
Perhaps, once again,
we are moving too fast.

833
00:34:47.810 --> 00:34:51.239
Let's go step by step.

834
00:34:51.239 --> 00:34:55.239
What is the state
of nature like?

835
00:34:55.239 --> 00:34:55.810
Alvis.

836
00:34:55.810 --> 00:34:58.810
In addition to freedom and
equality, there is a law.

837
00:34:58.810 --> 00:34:59.320
Good.

838
00:34:59.320 --> 00:35:01.030
What else?

839
00:35:01.030 --> 00:35:02.290
There is punishment.

840
00:35:02.290 --> 00:35:05.590
In paragraph seven, he
says everyone has a right

841
00:35:05.590 --> 00:35:08.830
to punish the transgressors
of that law to such a degree

842
00:35:08.830 --> 00:35:10.720
as may hinder its violation.

843
00:35:10.720 --> 00:35:11.980
Very good, Sophie.

844
00:35:11.980 --> 00:35:15.100
I think we will see that
our objection to bias

845
00:35:15.100 --> 00:35:18.130
is itself based on a sense
that there is a law of nature.

846
00:35:18.130 --> 00:35:20.780
How does Locke characterize
this statement?

847
00:35:20.780 --> 00:35:25.750
He says in paragraph nine that
this will seem a very strange

848
00:35:25.750 --> 00:35:27.880
doctrine to some.

849
00:35:27.880 --> 00:35:28.780
Excellent.

850
00:35:28.780 --> 00:35:32.140
Does it seem strange to you?

851
00:35:32.140 --> 00:35:35.350
Well, not any more strange
than the state of nature.

852
00:35:35.350 --> 00:35:37.420
Why is the state
of nature strange?

853
00:35:37.420 --> 00:35:40.720
So nature is what you
experience when you go on a hike

854
00:35:40.720 --> 00:35:42.530
or go camping.

855
00:35:42.530 --> 00:35:45.670
What Locke is describing
never really existed.

856
00:35:45.670 --> 00:35:48.080
What do you think
existed in its place?

857
00:35:48.080 --> 00:35:50.020
So humans evolved
from humanoids,

858
00:35:50.020 --> 00:35:51.670
and they lived in tribes.

859
00:35:51.670 --> 00:35:53.230
They weren't by themselves.

860
00:35:53.230 --> 00:35:54.070
Right, Patch.

861
00:35:54.070 --> 00:35:57.640
But what is the basis of the
rule of the tribe leader?

862
00:35:57.640 --> 00:35:58.600
Power.

863
00:35:58.600 --> 00:36:01.300
But isn't this exactly what
Locke said at the beginning?

864
00:36:01.300 --> 00:36:03.640
We can have tribal
rule based on power,

865
00:36:03.640 --> 00:36:06.520
or we can have an alternative,
which Locke provides.

866
00:36:06.520 --> 00:36:10.300
Besides, don't you think
human beings' natural state is

867
00:36:10.300 --> 00:36:13.240
freedom and equality?

868
00:36:13.240 --> 00:36:14.230
That all makes sense.

869
00:36:14.230 --> 00:36:18.280
But then why does Locke call
it a very strange doctrine?

870
00:36:18.280 --> 00:36:19.510
To some.

871
00:36:19.510 --> 00:36:22.300
See, I think that will
find that what is required

872
00:36:22.300 --> 00:36:26.140
is not so much a new doctrine,
say a learned latent structure

873
00:36:26.140 --> 00:36:30.010
for algorithmic bias mitigation
or domain adaptation,

874
00:36:30.010 --> 00:36:33.010
but a reflection
on what doctrine

875
00:36:33.010 --> 00:36:35.830
some find strange
and others do not.

876
00:36:35.830 --> 00:36:40.270
What's strange to us actually
contradicts our own biases.

877
00:36:40.270 --> 00:36:44.050
Psychologically, we tend
to resist those things

878
00:36:44.050 --> 00:36:47.960
because they point out
our biases to ourselves.

879
00:36:47.960 --> 00:36:50.290
Oh, I see.

880
00:36:50.290 --> 00:36:55.180
What we find strange is an
indication of our own bias.

881
00:36:55.180 --> 00:36:57.310
That's what Locke is saying.

882
00:36:57.310 --> 00:36:58.960
Thank you, Professor Knowles.

883
00:36:58.960 --> 00:37:01.240
And I realize that he
defends this doctrine

884
00:37:01.240 --> 00:37:03.790
by arguing that governments
can punish foreigners

885
00:37:03.790 --> 00:37:06.490
who break a law,
which is related

886
00:37:06.490 --> 00:37:09.430
to the example in
paragraph 14, when he says

887
00:37:09.430 --> 00:37:14.230
that by the state of nature
exists today, since all princes

888
00:37:14.230 --> 00:37:16.210
and rulers of
independent governments

889
00:37:16.210 --> 00:37:19.030
all through the world
are in a state of nature.

890
00:37:19.030 --> 00:37:22.570
Very good, and then he
brings in the next paragraph

891
00:37:22.570 --> 00:37:24.700
the weight of the writings
of Hooker, or shall

892
00:37:24.700 --> 00:37:26.770
we say the judicious Hooker.

893
00:37:26.770 --> 00:37:30.290
And he ends the chapter
with the idea of consent.

894
00:37:30.290 --> 00:37:34.845
So political society is made
legitimate only by consent.

895
00:37:34.845 --> 00:37:36.220
Well, since we
are short on time,

896
00:37:36.220 --> 00:37:39.010
I'll have to move on to
chapter five on property.

897
00:37:39.010 --> 00:37:40.690
But explore when
you can what he says

898
00:37:40.690 --> 00:37:44.200
about the state of war and
slavery in the Skip chapters.

899
00:37:44.200 --> 00:37:46.300
We will also come back
to other subtleties,

900
00:37:46.300 --> 00:37:48.580
like why he calls the
doctrine by everyone

901
00:37:48.580 --> 00:37:50.710
in the state of nature
has executive power

902
00:37:50.710 --> 00:37:53.410
this strange doctrine
in paragraph 13

903
00:37:53.410 --> 00:37:54.880
and, for that
matter, why there is

904
00:37:54.880 --> 00:37:59.440
no authority in the state of
nature besides each individual.

905
00:37:59.440 --> 00:38:03.810
So what are his key
arguments on property?

906
00:38:03.810 --> 00:38:05.280
Alyssa.

907
00:38:05.280 --> 00:38:09.300
One is that each of us has a
property in our own person.

908
00:38:09.300 --> 00:38:11.310
That's in paragraph 27.

909
00:38:11.310 --> 00:38:11.910
Good.

910
00:38:11.910 --> 00:38:15.690
And later in paragraph 27, that
we make things our property

911
00:38:15.690 --> 00:38:18.090
by mixing our labor
with things from nature.

912
00:38:18.090 --> 00:38:18.850
Very good.

913
00:38:18.850 --> 00:38:19.350
Yeah.

914
00:38:19.350 --> 00:38:20.860
I like the acorn example.

915
00:38:20.860 --> 00:38:23.910
By picking up an
acorn, we make it ours.

916
00:38:23.910 --> 00:38:25.500
I guess that applies
to fruit, too.

917
00:38:25.500 --> 00:38:27.330
[LAUGHTER]

918
00:38:27.330 --> 00:38:31.410
And he says in paragraph
26 that the Earth is ours

919
00:38:31.410 --> 00:38:33.510
for the support and
comfort of our being

920
00:38:33.510 --> 00:38:35.790
and that we're given
reason to use it well.

921
00:38:35.790 --> 00:38:37.950
That brings me back to
my point from before.

922
00:38:37.950 --> 00:38:40.140
Someone isn't using it
well if they're rich

923
00:38:40.140 --> 00:38:41.250
and they hoard things.

924
00:38:41.250 --> 00:38:43.260
Well, you can only
hoard so much.

925
00:38:43.260 --> 00:38:44.910
Like, who needs 10 Ferraris?

926
00:38:44.910 --> 00:38:46.440
I do.

927
00:38:46.440 --> 00:38:47.580
Ha, ha.

928
00:38:47.580 --> 00:38:49.080
Ha, ha, yourself.

929
00:38:49.080 --> 00:38:51.540
The point is that you cannot
hoard because your things will

930
00:38:51.540 --> 00:38:52.320
spoil.

931
00:38:52.320 --> 00:38:53.040
Yes.

932
00:38:53.040 --> 00:38:54.900
That is why he talks
about money somewhere.

933
00:38:54.900 --> 00:38:57.060
Money doesn't go bad.

934
00:38:57.060 --> 00:38:59.220
Yes, in paragraph 36.

935
00:38:59.220 --> 00:39:02.040
But you'll see that he
says in paragraph 31

936
00:39:02.040 --> 00:39:04.590
that everyone can take as
much as he wants from nature,

937
00:39:04.590 --> 00:39:06.300
but there is a limitation.

938
00:39:06.300 --> 00:39:09.360
What is that limitation that
prevents us from accumulating

939
00:39:09.360 --> 00:39:12.030
so much that things spoil?

940
00:39:12.030 --> 00:39:15.300
Well, what is also present
in nature besides things

941
00:39:15.300 --> 00:39:16.950
you can take?

942
00:39:16.950 --> 00:39:17.760
Oh, yes.

943
00:39:17.760 --> 00:39:19.140
The law of reason.

944
00:39:19.140 --> 00:39:22.080
It's stupid to hoard because
then you're just wasting stuff,

945
00:39:22.080 --> 00:39:23.820
and that's against
the law of reason.

946
00:39:23.820 --> 00:39:27.690
But once there is money, you
can hoard it without spoiling.

947
00:39:27.690 --> 00:39:30.480
So why isn't that wrong to do?

948
00:39:30.480 --> 00:39:34.480
Because you put
your labor into it?

949
00:39:34.480 --> 00:39:36.360
But what if you're just lucky?

950
00:39:36.360 --> 00:39:39.190
Or what if you inherited it?

951
00:39:39.190 --> 00:39:41.370
OK if I answer this one?

952
00:39:41.370 --> 00:39:45.300
It's because someone else
put their labor into it,

953
00:39:45.300 --> 00:39:50.070
therefore they can give
however they choose.

954
00:39:50.070 --> 00:39:50.790
Excellent.

955
00:39:50.790 --> 00:39:51.960
Thanks.

956
00:39:51.960 --> 00:39:54.810
Do you recall how much
Locke says labor increases

957
00:39:54.810 --> 00:39:58.020
the value of what is in nature?

958
00:39:58.020 --> 00:39:59.710
100 times in paragraph 40.

959
00:39:59.710 --> 00:40:02.100
1,000 times in paragraph 43.

960
00:40:02.100 --> 00:40:05.100
Both 10 and 100 times
in paragraph 37.

961
00:40:05.100 --> 00:40:07.230
And since we mentioned
paragraph 40,

962
00:40:07.230 --> 00:40:09.060
we should point out
his famous statement

963
00:40:09.060 --> 00:40:10.590
in the subsequent one.

964
00:40:10.590 --> 00:40:13.380
A king of a large and
fruitful territory in America

965
00:40:13.380 --> 00:40:17.280
feeds, lodges, and is clad worse
than a day laborer in England.

966
00:40:17.280 --> 00:40:19.890
So whatever the
factor of increase is,

967
00:40:19.890 --> 00:40:23.250
the point is not just that
labor is almost all the value

968
00:40:23.250 --> 00:40:26.160
in property but that
by protecting property

969
00:40:26.160 --> 00:40:28.800
we will all benefit
with many good things.

970
00:40:28.800 --> 00:40:30.330
Yes, like this university.

971
00:40:30.330 --> 00:40:31.680
[CHUCKLES]

972
00:40:31.680 --> 00:40:32.580
Exactly.

973
00:40:32.580 --> 00:40:35.250
I still don't think it's fair
that some people have so much

974
00:40:35.250 --> 00:40:36.910
more than others.

975
00:40:36.910 --> 00:40:39.030
But we all benefit.

976
00:40:39.030 --> 00:40:41.100
Besides, if it weren't
for this system,

977
00:40:41.100 --> 00:40:43.560
you wouldn't even be able
to get that broken-down Kia

978
00:40:43.560 --> 00:40:44.790
that you dream of.

979
00:40:44.790 --> 00:40:45.300
Oh.

980
00:40:45.300 --> 00:40:46.010
Ha, ha.

981
00:40:46.010 --> 00:40:49.140
[LAUGHTER]

982
00:40:49.140 --> 00:40:51.510
But even if the pie
gets bigger and bigger,

983
00:40:51.510 --> 00:40:53.280
some people will
still control others

984
00:40:53.280 --> 00:40:54.850
because they have
a bigger piece,

985
00:40:54.850 --> 00:40:58.320
making those people unhappy.

986
00:40:58.320 --> 00:41:00.540
Or do you mean less free?

987
00:41:00.540 --> 00:41:04.470
If so, it seems that AI doesn't
solve the problem at all.

988
00:41:04.470 --> 00:41:07.020
Of course, some people
do think that it can,

989
00:41:07.020 --> 00:41:08.940
that mathematization
of all things

990
00:41:08.940 --> 00:41:11.880
can lead to a sort
of techno utopia.

991
00:41:11.880 --> 00:41:13.840
Locke is giving us a choice.

992
00:41:13.840 --> 00:41:17.460
We can be controlled by
algorithms designed by smart

993
00:41:17.460 --> 00:41:21.270
but unworldly people working
long hours in some basement--

994
00:41:21.270 --> 00:41:23.520
or let's call it a cave--

995
00:41:23.520 --> 00:41:26.160
or we can be allowed
our own choice.

996
00:41:26.160 --> 00:41:28.140
Of course, that choice
should be the product

997
00:41:28.140 --> 00:41:31.350
of thoughtful reflection,
particularly on our own biases.

998
00:41:31.350 --> 00:41:35.500
But AI could give more
freedom, couldn't it?

999
00:41:35.500 --> 00:41:36.660
I see what you're saying.

1000
00:41:36.660 --> 00:41:39.300
Certainly, it can bring
benefits in health care

1001
00:41:39.300 --> 00:41:42.720
and transportation and make
many things more efficient.

1002
00:41:42.720 --> 00:41:45.180
But intrinsically it
can't solve the problem

1003
00:41:45.180 --> 00:41:47.910
of bias even in itself.

1004
00:41:47.910 --> 00:41:49.620
Actually, it's even worse.

1005
00:41:49.620 --> 00:41:52.620
Those who develop and
therefore control AI

1006
00:41:52.620 --> 00:41:56.004
will build AI systems biased
for their own benefit.

1007
00:41:56.004 --> 00:41:56.504
Hmm.

1008
00:41:56.504 --> 00:41:57.480
Hmm.

1009
00:41:57.480 --> 00:41:59.910
Having said that, I
realized that we somehow

1010
00:41:59.910 --> 00:42:02.940
seem to have gotten
distracted from understanding

1011
00:42:02.940 --> 00:42:07.500
how natural right can help
us to address bias in AI.

1012
00:42:07.500 --> 00:42:10.740
And we only have a few
minutes of class left.

1013
00:42:10.740 --> 00:42:13.720
I was wondering when you
were going to realize that.

1014
00:42:13.720 --> 00:42:16.620
Well, you convinced
us that all AI systems

1015
00:42:16.620 --> 00:42:18.120
will be biased in
some dimensions

1016
00:42:18.120 --> 00:42:20.050
no matter what you do.

1017
00:42:20.050 --> 00:42:23.580
But then you also
convinced us that property

1018
00:42:23.580 --> 00:42:25.600
should be respected.

1019
00:42:25.600 --> 00:42:28.650
So, what do we do about bias?

1020
00:42:28.650 --> 00:42:29.160
Yeah.

1021
00:42:29.160 --> 00:42:31.320
And how should we
punish people who write

1022
00:42:31.320 --> 00:42:33.540
unacceptably biased AI systems?

1023
00:42:33.540 --> 00:42:37.990
Or reward them for making
acceptably biased AI systems.

1024
00:42:37.990 --> 00:42:39.630
I mean unbiased AI systems.

1025
00:42:39.630 --> 00:42:41.100
[LAUGHTER]

1026
00:42:41.100 --> 00:42:41.610
Yeah.

1027
00:42:41.610 --> 00:42:43.260
We can't reward
or punish people,

1028
00:42:43.260 --> 00:42:45.690
according to Locke's system.

1029
00:42:45.690 --> 00:42:48.330
On the contrary,
we can reward them

1030
00:42:48.330 --> 00:42:50.490
by buying their
products if we like them

1031
00:42:50.490 --> 00:42:52.650
or punish them by not
buying their products

1032
00:42:52.650 --> 00:42:54.070
if we don't like them.

1033
00:42:54.070 --> 00:42:56.310
So transparency is the key?

1034
00:42:56.310 --> 00:42:59.850
Yes, if people are willing
to pay for transparency.

1035
00:42:59.850 --> 00:43:02.910
You see, the law of
nature addresses that too.

1036
00:43:02.910 --> 00:43:04.860
And in most
contemporary approaches,

1037
00:43:04.860 --> 00:43:06.810
there is an
assumption, one which

1038
00:43:06.810 --> 00:43:08.730
we have been
addressing today too,

1039
00:43:08.730 --> 00:43:11.550
that bias is bad and
should be eradicated

1040
00:43:11.550 --> 00:43:13.920
by technical mathematical means.

1041
00:43:13.920 --> 00:43:17.220
We now understand that a deeper
reflection on the law of nature

1042
00:43:17.220 --> 00:43:20.070
and the law of reason is
needed, but we engineers

1043
00:43:20.070 --> 00:43:22.890
needed to see the limitations
of the mathematical or computer

1044
00:43:22.890 --> 00:43:25.200
science approach
first before we turn

1045
00:43:25.200 --> 00:43:26.830
to a broader or deeper one.

1046
00:43:26.830 --> 00:43:27.330
Yeah.

1047
00:43:27.330 --> 00:43:30.870
I think I understand
this now, but it still

1048
00:43:30.870 --> 00:43:32.760
hasn't changed one thing.

1049
00:43:32.760 --> 00:43:34.020
I want a Ferrari.

1050
00:43:34.020 --> 00:43:36.060
[SCOFFING, CHUCKLING]

1051
00:43:36.060 --> 00:43:40.410
The point is under the Lockean
system we are all better off--

1052
00:43:40.410 --> 00:43:42.000
happier, we could say--

1053
00:43:42.000 --> 00:43:44.340
because algorithms
with unacceptable

1054
00:43:44.340 --> 00:43:47.760
bias will go away because
no one will buy them.

1055
00:43:47.760 --> 00:43:50.580
You're starting to understand.

1056
00:43:50.580 --> 00:43:53.100
Earlier, you mentioned
utilitarianism,

1057
00:43:53.100 --> 00:43:56.260
and you showed not only
that AI is tied up in it

1058
00:43:56.260 --> 00:43:59.370
but that it doesn't treat
the human things well.

1059
00:43:59.370 --> 00:44:01.140
But what I didn't
really understand

1060
00:44:01.140 --> 00:44:04.440
is that we still need to
make utilitarian choices,

1061
00:44:04.440 --> 00:44:06.990
like allocation of resources.

1062
00:44:06.990 --> 00:44:10.410
Could you show us how to
think about that necessity

1063
00:44:10.410 --> 00:44:13.110
within the realm
of human things?

1064
00:44:13.110 --> 00:44:14.460
Yes, I can.

1065
00:44:14.460 --> 00:44:17.730
We did say that utilitarianism
as a form of thought

1066
00:44:17.730 --> 00:44:20.130
tends to give an incomplete
account of human concerns

1067
00:44:20.130 --> 00:44:21.930
and passions, no
matter how much we

1068
00:44:21.930 --> 00:44:24.180
strive to make the
mathematical model complete.

1069
00:44:24.180 --> 00:44:27.600
But we did indicate that
engineering is utilitarian

1070
00:44:27.600 --> 00:44:31.110
and that it always strives
to optimize some function.

1071
00:44:31.110 --> 00:44:33.630
How we should think about
that for human benefit

1072
00:44:33.630 --> 00:44:36.160
would be the subject
for next class.

1073
00:44:36.160 --> 00:44:36.660
[BELL RINGS]

1074
00:44:36.660 --> 00:44:40.010
[MUSIC PLAYING]

1075
00:44:40.010 --> 00:45:45.000


